+# Agent Note: Launcher-owned managed composition
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-24-launcher-owned-managed-composition.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The ordinary profile launcher deliberately gives a local user three composition authorities: the profile patch, the Harness-home patch, and repeated `--patch` files. It also discovers a writable preset root, watches both user patch files, and lets `dsh plugin` change a profile's package graph. Those are useful personal-development contracts, but they let an employee change which code receives company credentials or re-enable a network capability that an enterprise Launcher intended to withhold. The [configuration-source ownership decision](2026-08-04-configuration-source-ownership.md) also makes stored settings outrank entry configuration, so hiding a settings page does not pin an endpoint.
+
+## Decision
+
+The `session-turn-outline` projection is explicitly allowlisted for local chat navigation; it adds no persistence backend or network capability.
+
+The `enterprise-managed` profile is reserved for an exact inherited-process signal, `DSH_ENTERPRISE_MANAGED_BOOT=ensou-dsh-launcher/v1`. The signal and profile must appear together; a mismatch fails instead of entering ordinary boot. Environment files cannot supply the signal because the layered environment loader already excludes every `DSH_` name from discovered files.
+
+Managed boot initializes the profile only to retain the existing bundle resolver, requires the exact installation-owned base and Web bundle tuple, ignores the profile and Harness-home patch files, refuses `--patch`, and opens no user-patch watcher. Its Loader root is a fixed file under the installed CLI package rather than the writable profile directory, so profile-local `node_modules` cannot shadow allowlisted package names. Plugin management and config dumps are unavailable for the reserved profile.
+
+Before Loader mount, the managed host builds a deeply frozen, host-branded exact specifier-to-entry table from the resolved base/Web source installation and the digest-authorized host and preset rows. Package manifests and managed config roots must be physical canonical paths in that installation. Duplicate specifiers, unknown packages, cross-bundle equivocation, outside-installation targets, reparse config roots, and profile, home, cwd, user, or project resolution all fail closed. Root Include, Loader-created children, and PresetTree import only the stored canonical URLs. Managed mode never calls the writable profile `node_modules` heal path; ordinary mode retains it.
+
+The Launcher must supply matching `DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL` and `DEEPSEEK_SEARCH_BASE_URL` values with the exact form `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/v1`, plus one canonical 32-byte base64url `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`. Managed boot accepts those values only from the inherited process. The settings provider and alternate adapter are disabled, and the pinned DeepSeek adapter names only that inherited local bearer, so neither a stored settings section nor a discovered environment file can redirect model traffic away from the Launcher proxy. Its advertised model catalog contains only `deepseek-v4-flash`, which is also the fixed default model, and both the route and catalog entry use a fixed `maxTokens: 8192` output budget. The control plane must configure `Gateway:AllowedModel` to the exact value `deepseek-v4-flash` and enforce a request `max_tokens` ceiling of 8192; the catalog is intentionally not an authorization mechanism, so the Gateway is the final model-id and output-budget enforcement point.
+
+The final host entry list has an exact id-to-package allowlist and a normalized full-composition SHA-256. Unknown, missing, renamed, nested, or reconfigured rows fail before Loader boot. The sandbox default and hard maximum are both fixed to `workspace-write`, and `lockWorkspaceRoot: true` pins every resolved call to the Launcher-owned runtime working directory (`process.cwd()`). A restored session whose canonical header cwd differs from that root is rejected before fs, bash, pwsh, terminal, or str-replace execution. Approval is fixed to `ask`, and the permission table contains only the `workspace-write` preset. The maximum is applied after explicit per-call requests and the last durable `sandbox/mode` event, so an approved request, a UI-originated event, or an old restored `danger-full-access` session cannot widen execution above `workspace-write`. The runtime working directory is first checked against `process.cwd()` and then replaced with a fixed marker only while calculating the digest, so a legitimate workspace-dependent value does not make a release valid only from its build directory. Managed policy disables settings, alternate model adapters, telemetry, Web search and fetch, dynamic Cordis runners, plugin inventory and authoring UI, client HMR, settings UI, the model selector, and the permission selector. The current renderer, official-brand, attachment, session-reference, file-reference, and reference UI rows remain explicitly allowlisted and digest-pinned; the official brand stays enabled. The upstream persistent PowerShell package is absent from the managed row and exact-resolution tables, and the enterprise preset retains the bounded one-shot `dsh-tool-pwsh`. Shipped session persistence remains JSONL; the schema-17 `session-persistence-sqlite` provider is not mounted, while `session-query-sqlite` retains its in-memory `openAt: never` configuration and never opens SQLite. The host skill registry remains enabled. The Launcher must supply `ENSOU_DSH_ENTERPRISE_SKILLS_ROOT` from the inherited process as the exact canonical fully-qualified spelling of one existing directory; NUL, lexical aliases, missing paths, non-directories, and symbolic-link, junction, or other reparse redirection anywhere in the path fail before Loader boot. The host filesystem provider is re-enabled only as `includeDefaultRoots: false`, `customSkillDirs: [validatedRoot]`, `watch: false`, and `watchFollowSymlinks: false`, so project, user, default, and `DSH_BUNDLED_SKILL_DIR` roots remain invisible. The fixed host badge provider is the only packaged exception. The host `tool-skill` row stays disabled, while the enterprise preset mounts that Consumer alone. The preset roster has one installation-owned `system` root, `includeUserRoot: false`, and one `enterprise` preset. The complete recursive preset document, including every `config`, `disabled`, `group`, and `isolate` field, plus `preset.yml` metadata and the fixed Loader-root `cordis.yml`, each has a canonical SHA-256. The preset contains local workspace, shell, filesystem, skill consumption, planning, compaction, interaction, todo, and goal capabilities but no Web, fetch, search, alternate skill provider, subagent, workflow, or dynamic mount capability.
+
+Managed Web startup accepts exactly `--host 127.0.0.1 --port <port>`, with one canonical decimal port from 1 through 65535. Missing, repeated, reordered, aliased, additional, `--no-open`, or `--trusted-host` arguments fail before host boot. The managed overlay fixes `web-runtime.openBrowser` to false so the Launcher alone decides when to show the UI; ordinary profiles retain the Web app's browser handoff and existing argument behavior.
+
+Ordinary profiles keep their user layers, writable preset root, live watchers, overlays, plugin command, config dumps, and existing shipped preset roster. This decision specializes one reserved deployment entry instead of changing the personal product contract described by the [preset authoring decision](../bug-fix/2026-08-11-preset-authoring-agent-validates-its-own-composition.md).
+
+## Testing
+
+`apps/cli/tests/managed-boot.spec.ts` proves exact signal selection, overlay and auxiliary-command refusal, exact Web argument acceptance and real command-dispatch rejection, profile/home/user-preset injection exclusion, profile-local package-shadow and installation-config junction negative cases, the one-model `deepseek-v4-flash` catalog and 8192-token budget, fixed `workspace-write` maximum/root lock/`ask` policy and single permission preset, runtime-working-directory digest normalization, disabled model and permission selectors, fixed roster configuration, disabled network and dynamic rows, host and preset unknown-row rejection, recursive preset `config`/`disabled`/`isolate` digest rejection, metadata and Loader-root digest rejection, absence of Web capabilities, and unchanged normal composition. Its skill cases accept one canonical existing directory; reject missing, relative, noncanonical, NUL, file, and reparse paths; pin the exact host provider and preset Consumer rows; and use the real registry, filesystem provider, catalog, and `skill` tool to load a managed skill while project, user, agent, and bundled environment roots remain absent. App-boot and PresetTree tests additionally exercise the branded frozen resolution service, exact Loader-created child imports, duplicate/unknown/equivocal/outside/reparse rejection, and absence of ambient fallback. Sandbox-policy tests restore real JSONL sessions carrying both a final `danger-full-access` event and an outside cwd. The policy resolver rejects the outside root, while fs, bash, and pwsh tests pass independently reopened JSONL sessions through real tool execution and prove their provider/executor boundaries are not reached; terminal and shared execution tests prove the effective mode remains at most `workspace-write`. Ordinary policy tests prove deployments without a maximum or root lock keep their prior behavior. The existing argument, telemetry, and profile suites remain the regression checks for personal mode. `verify-cordis-config` validates the managed preset through the same config-file inventory as every shipped composition.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Hide the plugin and settings UI while retaining ordinary composition.** Rejected because local files and loopback APIs remain authorities even when their controls are not rendered, and model adapters layer stored settings over entry configuration.
+
+**Trust only row ids.** Rejected because an allowed id could name another package, acquire nested rows, or change a security-relevant config. The package-name map provides a precise diagnostic while the normalized digest pins the complete resolved upstream entry data.
+
+**Use the managed profile directory as the Loader root.** Rejected because Node resolves a profile-local package before the installation fallback. Ignoring patch files does not prevent same-name package shadowing.
+
+**Remove personal customization repository-wide.** Rejected because personal profiles intentionally support local plugin development and live patching. A reserved profile and exact Launcher signal keep that contract intact.
+
+## Consequences
+
+An upstream bundle or managed preset change cannot enter an employee release without updating and reviewing the explicit capability policy and composition digest. This creates intentional update work and turns unexpected drift into a startup failure rather than silent capability expansion.
+
+Managed users cannot configure alternate providers, select another model, switch to `danger-full-access`, author presets, install plugins, or use Web search from this runtime. They can load the fixed badge and skills installed under the validated enterprise root, but cannot expose ambient project, user, agent, or bundled-environment skill directories. The enterprise skill tree is unwatched and must be installed before process start. Centrally approved capabilities require a signed runtime update that changes both the installed composition and its allowlist. Local conversations and workspaces remain under the Harness home; only composition and provider authority are locked.
+
+The signal is a mode selector, not a local hostile-user security boundary. The same Windows user can invoke an ordinary profile directly, and managed-mode guarantees apply only when the signed Launcher starts the reserved profile through this controlled path. A user who can replace the signed runtime or Launcher can replace the policy as well, so the installer must keep those artifacts in its verified release set and apply operating-system write controls appropriate to the deployment. Before spawning DSH, the Launcher must clear `NODE_OPTIONS`, `NODE_PATH`, and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` from the child environment; that Node injection and TLS trust boundary is intentionally outside this DSH patch.
