+# Agent Note: Managed runtime update checkpoint
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-14-managed-runtime-update-checkpoint.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The Windows Launcher owns an authenticated named pipe and exact child process identity for runtime replacement, but the DSH process does not connect to that channel. A process stop inferred from HTTP health or Agent status cannot prove that request admission stopped, newly created Agents stayed fenced, credential and settings writes settled, or every live Session reached its persistence listener.
+
+## Decision
+
+The CLI activates `ensou.dsh.runtime-update.v1` only for the Launcher-authenticated `enterprise-direct-local` profile or an exact Personal Web opt-in marker. The inherited pipe path must be the canonical name derived from the inherited runtime instance UUID; ordinary profiles and partial or cross-profile bootstrap state fail closed.
+
+Drain closes Web admission and awaits accepted HTTP requests. It acquires each live Agent's real maintenance lease and registers its synchronous `agent/created` guard before profile entries mount, so a new Agent is fenced before its driver can start. Session creation and append events invalidate an otherwise ready checkpoint. The coordinator repeats credential, settings, and per-Session durability barriers until that guarded live set is stable.
+
+Credential providers declare whether they support a durability checkpoint; the local provider follows its serialized operation tail until stable. Settings owns its write queues and follows every tail. `SessionStore.flush` must report at least one persistence listener for every live Session. A missing participant or failed barrier reopens Web admission, releases the exact maintenance leases, and reports `resumed` rather than fabricating `persistenceFlushed`.
+
+Resume and pipe disconnect restore only those held leases and the same Web endpoint. Automatic recovery from a failed checkpoint and an explicit Resume for the same operation join one restore owned by that drain generation; repeated Resume is idempotent, while a different operation, a later drain generation, or disposal cannot inherit the result. If cancellation races an accepted HTTP response that has not finished, a fresh listener generation rebinds the retained port while the old generation continues draining that response and remains a barrier for a later drain; only whole-tree teardown terminates it. Shutdown is admitted only for the operation that already received a ready checkpoint; the receipt is written before the existing whole-tree `ProcessShutdown` owner runs. The protocol parser limits one line to 8 KiB, admits exact fields and canonical UUIDs, and never logs request bodies, credentials, browser state, or provider traffic.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Treat HTTP health loss as a drain.** Closing or losing the browser endpoint says nothing about Agent activity or write-behind persistence, so the Launcher could replace files while the child still owns mutable state.
+
+**Snapshot idle Agents and flush their Sessions once.** A Session or Agent created while the snapshot flushes can escape it. The synchronous creation lease and dirty-revision loop make that overlap structural instead of timing-dependent.
+
+**Dispose the whole tree on drain.** A failed checkpoint or cancelled update could not resume the retained child, violating the Host protocol's retryable recovery path. Maintenance leases preserve queued work without beginning another model request.
+
+**Assume every credential provider is flushable.** That would reinterpret an absent capability as success. The base provider returns unsupported, and only an owner that can await its writes returns true.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Managed drain temporarily closes the browser listener and upgraded streams; resume rebinds the exact retained port without waiting for or cancelling an accepted long response, so browser clients reconnect through their ordinary path. A repeated drain still waits that response. Active Agent work is allowed to finish without timeout cancellation, while new prompts remain queued behind maintenance. A persistence failure makes the update retryable and leaves the runtime serving again. The default CLI path has no pipe client, no admission pause, and no lifecycle behavior change.
